        <h1 class=3D"page-header">
<span>Giovanni Bellini's "Piet=C3=A0" Restored and Pietro Perugino=E2=80=99=
s =E2=80=9CMan of Sorrows=E2=80=9D</span>
</h1>

 =20
<article data-history-node-id=3D"7726271" class=3D"exhibitions full clearfi=
x">

 =20
   =20

 =20
  <div class=3D"content">
   =20
   =20
            <div class=3D"field field--name-field-display-date field--type-=
string field--label-hidden field--item">Through April 19, 2026</div>
     =20
            <div class=3D"field field--name-body field--type-text-with-summ=
ary field--label-hidden field--item"><div class=3D"row" style=3D"margin-bot=
tom:20px;"><div class=3D"col-sm-6" style=3D"margin-bottom:10px;"><img class=
=3D"img-responsive copyprevention-processed" src=3D"https://www.themorgan.o=
rg/sites/default/files/exhibitions/Bellini-Pieta.jpg" width=3D"550" height=
=3D"367" loading=3D"lazy"><p class=3D"small">Giovanni Bellini (1424/26=E2=
=80=931516), <em>Piet=C3=A0</em> (also known as <em>Dead Christ Supported b=
y Angels</em>) (ca. 1470). Photography by Matteo De Fina, courtesy of Museo=
 della Citt=C3=A0 =E2=80=9CLuigi Tonini,=E2=80=9D Rimini.</p></div><div cla=
ss=3D"col-sm-6"><img class=3D"img-responsive copyprevention-processed" src=
=3D"https://www.themorgan.org/sites/default/files/exhibitions/perugino.jpg"=
 width=3D"550" height=3D"523" loading=3D"lazy"><div class=3D"copyprevention=
-transparent-gif" style=3D"position: absolute; left: 15px; top: 0px"><img s=
rc=3D"https://www.themorgan.org/modules/contrib/copyprevention/transparent.=
gif" style=3D"width: 550px !important; height: 523px !important;"></div><p =
class=3D"small">Pietro Perugino (ca. 1450=E2=80=931523). Man of Sorrows (14=
95). By courtesy of the Ministry of Culture =E2=80=93 National Museums of P=
erugia =E2=80=93 Regional Directorate of National Museums of Umbria, Nation=
al Gallery of Umbria, Perugia. Photo credit: Haltadefinizione=C2=AE</p></di=
v></div><p>The Morgan is honored to display two Italian Renaissance masterp=
ieces: Giovanni Bellini's <em>Piet=C3=A0</em> (also known as <em>Dead Chris=
t Supported by Angels,</em> ca. 1470) and Pietro Perugino=E2=80=99s <em>Man=
 of Sorrows</em> (1495).</p><p>The <em>Piet=C3=A0</em> by Giovanni Bellini =
(1424/26=E2=80=931516), on view in the United States for the first time, is=
 on loan from the Museo della Citt=C3=A0 in Rimini, Italy, following a comp=
rehensive conservation treatment made possible by Venetian Heritage, Inc. T=
his melancholy and powerful Piet=C3=A0 shows youthful angels contemplating =
the wounds of Christ=E2=80=99s dead body as they arrange it for veneration.=
 In contrast to other versions of the subject, the angels in this painting =
do not wail uncontrollably; instead, the work=E2=80=99s poignancy derives f=
rom their sad, pensive preparation of Christ=E2=80=99s body.</p><p>The <em>=
Man of Sorrows</em> by Pietro Perugino (ca. 1450=E2=80=931523), on loan fro=
m the Galleria Nazionale dell=E2=80=99Umbria, Perugia, also shows Christ wi=
thin his tomb, with his eyes closed and head bowed, displaying his wounds. =
The painting, which retains its original frame, was a part of an altarpiece=
 commissioned by the Decemviri, Perugia=E2=80=99s ten ruling magistrates, f=
or their chapel in the town hall. Its display at the Morgan is thanks to th=
e Friends of Umbria, a newly founded American organization supporting the N=
ational Museums of Umbria.</p><p>These exceptional loans are displayed in J=
. Pierpont Morgan=E2=80=99s Study within the Morgan=E2=80=99s historic libr=
ary, alongside highlights of Morgan=E2=80=99s own Renaissance collection, i=
ncluding paintings by Hans Memling and Perugino and sculptures by Antonio R=
ossellino.</p></div>
     =20
            <div class=3D"field field--name-field-credit-line field--type-t=
ext-long field--label-hidden field--item"><p><em>Giovanni Bellini=E2=80=99s=
 =E2=80=9CPiet=C3=A0=E2=80=9D Restored</em> is made possible by Venetian He=
ritage, Inc.</p><p>The exhibition of Perugino=E2=80=99s <em>Man of Sorrows<=
/em> at the Morgan is made possible by Brunello Cucinelli and Friends of Um=
bria; additional support is provided by Dr. Mitchell Levine.</p></div>
